    "['mpls', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls ip', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nmpls ip\\n##\\n\\nThe `mpls` command in Huawei configuration globally enables MPLS on the device. The equivalent Cisco IOS command is `mpls ip`, which enables MPLS forwarding globally and for IP routing protocols.', 'trans_template': 'mpls ip'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnels', 'parent_command': [], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nmpls traffic-eng tunnels\\n##\\n\\nThe `mpls te` command in Huawei configuration enables MPLS Traffic Engineering on the interface. The equivalent Cisco command is `mpls traffic-eng tunnels`, which performs the same function of enabling MPLS TE capabilities on the interface or globally.', 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng tunnels'}]]": 1,
    "['mpls te bandwidth max-reservable-bandwidth [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'mpls traffic-eng bandwidth max-reservable-bw 5000', 'parent_command': ['interface [parameter1]'], 'root': 'interface [parameter1]', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nmpls traffic-eng bandwidth max-reservable-bw 5000\\n##  \\n\\nThe Cisco equivalent command configures the maximum reservable bandwidth for MPLS Traffic Engineering on an interface, just like the Huawei command does. The key differences are:\\n- \"mpls te\" becomes \"mpls traffic-eng\" in Cisco\\n- \"bandwidth max-reservable-bandwidth\" becomes \"bandwidth max-reservable-bw\" in Cisco', 'trans_template': 'mpls traffic-eng bandwidth max-reservable-bw 5000'}]]": 1,
